# Who may publish and subscribe to what. Without this file every broker user could do everything (a compromised bridge could
# send commands to the units; the topics are the contract in lib/ac.ts).

# The web app: owns the unit list, sends commands and scan requests, reads what the bridge reports.
# It may also write the per-unit topics, only to clear their retained values when a unit is removed.
user app
topic write mhi/_devices
topic write mhi/_scan
topic write mhi/+/set
topic readwrite mhi/+/state
topic readwrite mhi/+/online
topic readwrite mhi/+/setup
topic read mhi/_found
topic write mhi/_health
# Home Assistant discovery (HA_DISCOVERY=1) and Home Assistant itself, if you connect it as this user
topic readwrite homeassistant/#

# The bridge: reads the unit list, commands and scan requests; reports what the adapters say. It can't command units itself.
user device
topic read mhi/_devices
topic read mhi/_scan
topic read mhi/+/set
topic write mhi/+/state
topic write mhi/+/online
topic write mhi/+/setup
topic write mhi/_found
# mhi/_bridge/online (the bridge's own presence, "0" as its last-will) matches mhi/+/online here and above: no separate rule. The app
# can also write it, as it can every mhi/+/online (to clear a removed unit's retained value); that wildcard can't be narrowed without that.
